<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Atlas's own file. Nobody publishes this one, and it is recorded as ours in standards/README.md.

  Every content model in sitemap.xsd and siteindex.xsd ends with

    <xsd:any namespace="##other" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded" processContents="strict"/>

  and the hreflang extension puts <xhtml:link> in exactly that position. `strict` means a validator
  has to find a global element declaration for a foreign element or the document is invalid, so a
  sitemap carrying alternates, validated against sitemap.xsd on its own, is reported as:

    Element '{http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml}link': No matching global element declaration
    available, but demanded by the strict wildcard.

  Making the XHTML schema merely findable does not answer that. The validator needs the namespace
  imported into the schema set it compiled, which is what this file is and all that it is. It
  declares no element, no type and no constraint, and it exists only because the protocol and the
  extension come from two publishers, so neither published schema imports the other.

  An index file is validated against siteindex.xsd directly rather than through this. It carries no
  foreign element, so it needs no import, and it could not have one anyway: siteindex.xsd and
  sitemap.xsd share a target namespace, and a second import of a namespace already imported is
  skipped, which was measured rather than assumed.
-->
<xsd:schema xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <xsd:import namespace="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9"
              schemaLocation="sitemap.xsd"/>
  <xsd:import namespace="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
              schemaLocation="xhtml1-strict.xsd"/>
</xsd:schema>
